    {
      "source_number": "43",
      "source_ref": "#/exercises/0",
      "source_pages": ["p0032#8"],
      "group_id": "g0",
      "domain": "x in R",
      "problem": "y=x^2-3x-4: x-axis intersections and approximate graph; a) x^2-3x-4<0; b) x^2-3x-4>0.",
      "shared_derivation": [
        "x^2-3x-4=(x+1)(x-4). Therefore the two distinct zeros are -1 and 4.",
        "The leading coefficient is 1>0, so the graph is an upward-opening parabola.",
        "Completing the square gives y=(x-3/2)^2-25/4. The symmetry axis is x=3/2 and the vertex is (3/2,-25/4).",
        "The y-axis intersection is (0,-4). Symmetry gives (3,-4), and additional exact plotting points are (1,-6), (2,-6), (-2,6), and (5,6).",
        "Starting in the upper left, the curve crosses the x-axis at -1, passes below the axis through (0,-4), reaches the vertex to the right of the y-axis, rises through (3,-4), crosses again at 4, and continues into the upper right."
      ],
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "43-coordinates",
          "method": "Set y=0 and factor the quadratic.",
          "exact_answer": ["(-1,0)", "(4,0)"]
        },
        {
          "id": "43-sketch",
          "method": "Use the opening direction, both zeros, the vertex, symmetry axis, and y-intercept. The following text sketch is qualitative, not a calibrated plot or an image artifact.",
          "exact_graph_features": {
            "opening": "upward",
            "axis": "x=3/2",
            "vertex": "(3/2,-25/4)",
            "x_intercepts": ["(-1,0)", "(4,0)"],
            "y_intercept": "(0,-4)"
          },
          "sketch_ascii": [
            "              y",
            "              ^",
            "   \\          |                       /",
            "    \\         |                      /",
            "-----*--------+--------------------*------> x",
            "   (-1,0)     O                 (4,0)",
            "      \\       |                  /",
            "       \\      * (0,-4)          /",
            "         \\    |              /",
            "           \\  |            /",
            "             \\|____*_____/",
            "                (3/2,-25/4)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "43a",
          "method": "Read where the upward parabola lies below the x-axis: strictly between its two intersections.",
          "graph_signs": "positive on (-infinity,-1); zero at -1; negative on (-1,4); zero at 4; positive on (4,infinity).",
          "exact_answer": "(-1,4)",
          "endpoint_rule": "Both roots are excluded because the inequality is strict."
        },
        {
          "id": "43b",
          "method": "Read where the upward parabola lies above the x-axis: both exterior intervals.",
          "exact_answer": "(-infinity,-1) union (4,infinity)",
          "endpoint_rule": "Both roots are excluded because the inequality is strict."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "44",
      "source_ref": "#/exercises/1",
      "source_pages": ["p0032#9", "p0033#1"],
      "group_id": "g0",
      "domain": "x in R",
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "44a",
          "problem": "x^2-x-90<0",
          "method": [
            "x^2-x-90=(x+9)(x-10). Ordered roots: -9<10.",
            "The upward parabola is negative between the roots and positive outside them."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "(-9,10)",
          "endpoint_rule": "Strict inequality excludes -9 and 10."
        },
        {
          "id": "44b",
          "problem": "6x^2-7x+2>0",
          "method": [
            "6x^2-7x+2=(2x-1)(3x-2). Ordered roots: 1/2<2/3.",
            "The product is positive when both factors are negative (x<1/2) or both positive (x>2/3); the upward graph has the same exterior-positive sign pattern."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "(-infinity,1/2) union (2/3,infinity)",
          "endpoint_rule": "Strict inequality excludes both roots."
        },
        {
          "id": "44c",
          "problem": "-x^2-2x+48<0",
          "method": [
            "-x^2-2x+48=-(x+8)(x-6). Ordered roots: -8<6.",
            "The parabola opens downward: negative outside the roots, positive between them. Equivalently, the leading minus sign reverses the product's signs."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "(-infinity,-8) union (6,infinity)",
          "endpoint_rule": "Strict inequality excludes -8 and 6."
        },
        {
          "id": "44d",
          "problem": "8x^2+10x-3>0",
          "method": [
            "8x^2+10x-3=(2x+3)(4x-1). Ordered roots: -3/2<1/4.",
            "The leading coefficient is positive, so the graph is positive on the two exterior intervals."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "(-infinity,-3/2) union (1/4,infinity)",
          "endpoint_rule": "Strict inequality excludes both roots."
        },
        {
          "id": "44e",
          "problem": "25x^2-10x+1>0",
          "method": [
            "25x^2-10x+1=(5x-1)^2.",
            "A square is positive everywhere except where its base vanishes. The graph touches the x-axis only at x=1/5."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "R excluding {1/5}",
          "interval_answer": "(-infinity,1/5) union (1/5,infinity)",
          "endpoint_rule": "The double root is excluded; it does not bound a negative interval."
        },
        {
          "id": "44f",
          "problem": "49x^2-28x+4<0",
          "method": [
            "49x^2-28x+4=(7x-2)^2>=0 for every real x.",
            "The double root x=2/7 gives zero, not a negative value."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "empty"
        },
        {
          "id": "44g",
          "problem": "-x^2-12x-100<0",
          "method": [
            "-x^2-12x-100=-(x+6)^2-64<=-64<0.",
            "The downward parabola has maximum -64 at x=-6 and therefore lies strictly below the x-axis everywhere."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "R"
        },
        {
          "id": "44h",
          "problem": "4x^2-4x+15<0",
          "method": [
            "4x^2-4x+15=4(x-1/2)^2+14>=14>0.",
            "The upward parabola has minimum 14 at x=1/2 and never takes a negative value."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "empty"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "45",
      "source_ref": "#/exercises/2",
      "source_pages": ["p0033#2"],
      "group_id": "g0",
      "domain": "x in R",
      "problem": "Solve 3x^2+7x-20>0 by both requested methods.",
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "45a",
          "method_name": "Quadratic graph",
          "method": [
            "For f(x)=3x^2+7x-20, a=3>0: the parabola opens upward.",
            "D=7^2-4*3*(-20)=289=17^2, so the roots are (-7-17)/6=-4 and (-7+17)/6=5/3, with -4<5/3.",
            "The x-intersections are (-4,0) and (5/3,0); the graph is below the axis between them and above it outside.",
            "For a fuller graph description, f(x)=3(x+7/6)^2-289/12, so the vertex is (-7/6,-289/12), below the x-axis.",
            "The condition f(x)>0 selects the two strictly exterior intervals."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "(-infinity,-4) union (5/3,infinity)"
        },
        {
          "id": "45b",
          "method_name": "Linear factors and positive-product conditions",
          "method": [
            "3x^2+7x-20=(3x-5)(x+4), since 12x-5x=7x and -5*4=-20.",
            "Case 1, both factors positive: 3x-5>0 and x+4>0 mean x>5/3 and x>-4. Their intersection is x>5/3.",
            "Case 2, both factors negative: 3x-5<0 and x+4<0 mean x<5/3 and x<-4. Their intersection is x<-4.",
            "One positive and one negative factor gives a negative product, and either zero factor gives zero. Neither satisfies the requested strict inequality.",
            "Take the union of the two same-sign cases."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "(-infinity,-4) union (5/3,infinity)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "46",
      "source_ref": "#/exercises/3",
      "source_pages": ["p0033#3"],
      "group_id": "g0",
      "domain": "x in R",
      "problem": "Prove x^2-4x+10>0 for every real x using both requested methods.",
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "46a",
          "method_name": "Quadratic graph",
          "method": [
            "For f(x)=x^2-4x+10, a=1>0, so the graph is an upward parabola.",
            "Its symmetry axis is x=-b/(2a)=2 and f(2)=4-8+10=6, so its vertex and minimum are (2,6) and 6.",
            "Thus every point of the graph has y>=6>0: the entire parabola lies strictly above the x-axis.",
            "Independently, D=(-4)^2-4*1*10=-24<0 confirms no real x-axis intersection."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "True for every x in R; solution set R; minimum value 6 at x=2."
        },
        {
          "id": "46b",
          "method_name": "Extract a binomial square",
          "method": [
            "x^2-4x+10=x^2-4x+4+6=(x-2)^2+6.",
            "For real x, (x-2)^2>=0, hence (x-2)^2+6>=6>0.",
            "At x=2 the square is zero but the original expression is 6, so even its minimum satisfies the strict inequality."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "True for every x in R; solution set R."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "47",
      "source_ref": "#/exercises/4",
      "source_pages": ["p0033#4"],
      "group_id": "g0",
      "domain": "Requested answers are integers x in Z; first solve each inequality over R.",
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "47a",
          "problem": "x^2-6x<0",
          "method": [
            "x^2-6x=x(x-6); its ordered roots are 0 and 6, and the upward quadratic is negative exactly for 0<x<6.",
            "Intersect that open real interval with Z."
          ],
          "real_solution_set": "(0,6)",
          "exact_answer": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],
          "endpoint_rule": "0 and 6 give zero and are excluded."
        },
        {
          "id": "47b",
          "problem": "x^2-4<=0",
          "method": [
            "(x-2)(x+2)<=0 gives -2<=x<=2.",
            "Intersect the closed interval with Z."
          ],
          "real_solution_set": "[-2,2]",
          "exact_answer": [-2, -1, 0, 1, 2],
          "endpoint_rule": "Both integer roots are included because equality is allowed."
        },
        {
          "id": "47c",
          "problem": "3x+x^2<=0",
          "method": [
            "x^2+3x=x(x+3); ordered roots are -3 and 0.",
            "The upward quadratic is nonpositive on [-3,0]. Intersect with Z."
          ],
          "real_solution_set": "[-3,0]",
          "exact_answer": [-3, -2, -1, 0],
          "endpoint_rule": "Both roots are included."
        },
        {
          "id": "47d",
          "problem": "x^2-5<0",
          "method": [
            "x^2<5 is equivalent to -sqrt(5)<x<sqrt(5).",
            "Since 2^2=4<5<9=3^2, 2<sqrt(5)<3. The possible integer magnitudes are therefore 0,1,2."
          ],
          "real_solution_set": "(-sqrt(5),sqrt(5))",
          "exact_answer": [-2, -1, 0, 1, 2],
          "endpoint_rule": "The irrational endpoints are excluded; integers +/-3 fail since their squares are 9."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "48",
      "source_ref": "#/exercises/5",
      "source_pages": ["p0033#5"],
      "group_id": "g0",
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "48a",
          "domain": "z in R",
          "problem": "5z(z+1)<=11z^2+1",
          "method": [
            "Expand the left side: 5z^2+5z<=11z^2+1.",
            "Subtract the left side from the right: 6z^2-5z+1>=0. No division by an expression depending on z is used.",
            "6z^2-5z+1=(3z-1)(2z-1), with ordered roots 1/3<1/2.",
            "The leading coefficient is positive, so nonnegativity holds outside the roots, including both roots."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "(-infinity,1/3] union [1/2,infinity)",
          "endpoint_rule": "Both endpoints satisfy equality in the original inequality."
        },
        {
          "id": "48b",
          "domain": "P in R; uppercase P is retained from the source.",
          "problem": "2P(P+1)<5P",
          "method": [
            "2P^2+2P<5P is equivalent to 2P^2-3P<0, then P(2P-3)<0.",
            "The roots are 0 and 3/2, and the upward quadratic is negative between them.",
            "Do not divide by P: its sign is not initially known, and P=0 must be checked rather than lost."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "(0,3/2)",
          "endpoint_rule": "Both roots yield equality and are excluded."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "49",
      "source_ref": "#/exercises/6",
      "source_pages": ["p0033#6"],
      "group_id": "g0",
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "49a",
          "problem": "Find the real domain of y=sqrt(2x-x^2).",
          "method": [
            "A real square root requires 2x-x^2>=0.",
            "2x-x^2=x(2-x), which is nonnegative exactly for 0<=x<=2.",
            "The endpoints give square root zero, which is permitted because the square root is not a denominator."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "[0,2]"
        },
        {
          "id": "49b",
          "problem": "Find the real domain of y=24/sqrt(x^2+4x+4).",
          "method": [
            "The radicand is x^2+4x+4=(x+2)^2, nonnegative for every real x.",
            "Since this square root is a denominator, it must also be nonzero: (x+2)^2>0, equivalent to x!=-2.",
            "sqrt((x+2)^2)=|x+2|, not x+2 on all of R. This preserves the branch and shows all x<-2 are allowed as well.",
            "At x=-2 the original denominator is zero and the formula is undefined."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "R excluding {-2}",
          "interval_answer": "(-infinity,-2) union (-2,infinity)",
          "original_denominator_holes": ["x=-2"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "50",
      "source_ref": "#/exercises/7",
      "source_pages": ["p0033#7"],
      "group_id": "g0",
      "domain": "x in R; both polynomial functions are defined on all R.",
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "50a",
          "problem": "When does y=-x^2+8x+2 have value greater than 9?",
          "method": [
            "-x^2+8x+2>9 is equivalent to -x^2+8x-7>0.",
            "Multiply by -1 and reverse the inequality: x^2-8x+7<0.",
            "x^2-8x+7=(x-1)(x-7); the upward shifted expression is negative between 1 and 7.",
            "Graphically, the original downward parabola intersects the horizontal line y=9 at x=1 and x=7 and lies above that line between them."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "(1,7)",
          "endpoint_rule": "At either endpoint y=9, not greater than 9."
        },
        {
          "id": "50b",
          "problem": "When does y=2x^2+x-6 have value less than 4?",
          "method": [
            "2x^2+x-6<4 is equivalent to 2x^2+x-10<0.",
            "2x^2+x-10=(2x+5)(x-2), with ordered roots -5/2<2.",
            "The upward shifted parabola is negative between its roots. Equivalently, the original graph is below the horizontal line y=4 there."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "(-5/2,2)",
          "endpoint_rule": "At either endpoint y=4, not less than 4."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "51",
      "source_ref": "#/exercises/8",
      "source_pages": ["p0033#8", "p0034#1"],
      "group_id": "g0",
      "domain": "x in R; real coefficients a,b,c with a!=0; D=b^2-4ac.",
      "original_figure_input": {
        "path": "ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/8a/figures/fig-12.png",
        "visual_access_count": 1,
        "upper_left_observation": "The row labeled a>0 and column labeled D>0 show an upward parabola crossing the x-axis twice, with its middle arc below the axis and both tails above.",
        "lower_right_observation": "The row labeled a<0 and column labeled D<0 show a downward parabola with its vertex and both branches strictly below the x-axis, and no crossing or tangency.",
        "use_boundary": "Original solving input only. No render, candidate figure, old screenshot, or figure-approval review."
      },
      "root_order": {
        "convention": "In block 1 define x1 to be the smaller real root and x2 the larger, so x1<x2.",
        "formulas_for_block1": ["x1=(-b-sqrt(D))/(2a)", "x2=(-b+sqrt(D))/(2a)"],
        "reason": "Here a>0 and D>0, hence x2-x1=sqrt(D)/a>0.",
        "label_independent_form": "Let L=min(the two real roots), U=max(the two real roots). Exterior means x<L or x>U; interior means L<=x<=U.",
        "ambiguity_handling": "The printed statement names the roots but does not impose an order. The explicit smaller/larger convention and equivalent min/max formulation make the mathematical sets unambiguous; no HOLD is needed. Do not infer any numerical root coordinates from the schematic figure."
      },
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "51-1a",
          "problem": "ax^2+bx+c>0, a>0, D>0",
          "method": [
            "Read the upper-left original panel: the upward curve is above the x-axis strictly outside its two crossings.",
            "With x1<x2, f(x)=a(x-x1)(x-x2). Since a>0, both factors have the same sign precisely for x<x1 or x>x2.",
            "At either root f(x)=0, which does not satisfy >0."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "(-infinity,x1) union (x2,infinity), where x1<x2",
          "order_independent_answer": "(-infinity,L) union (U,infinity)"
        },
        {
          "id": "51-1b",
          "problem": "ax^2+bx+c<=0, a>0, D>0",
          "method": [
            "Read the same upper-left original panel: the graph lies below the x-axis between the two crossings and equals zero at the crossings.",
            "For x1<x<x2, the factors (x-x1) and (x-x2) have opposite signs; at x1 and x2 the product is zero.",
            "Nonpositivity therefore includes the entire closed middle interval."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "[x1,x2], where x1<x2",
          "order_independent_answer": "[L,U]"
        },
        {
          "id": "51-2a",
          "problem": "ax^2+bx+c>=0, a<0, D<0",
          "method": [
            "Read the lower-right original panel: the entire downward parabola is strictly below the x-axis.",
            "Algebraically, f(x)=a(x+b/(2a))^2-D/(4a). The square term is <=0 because a<0.",
            "The vertex value -D/(4a) is strictly negative: -D>0 and 4a<0. Thus f(x)<=-D/(4a)<0 for every real x.",
            "There are no real roots when D<0, so equality at zero is impossible as well."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "empty"
        },
        {
          "id": "51-2b",
          "problem": "ax^2+bx+c<0, a<0, D<0",
          "method": [
            "Read the same lower-right original panel: every point is below the x-axis.",
            "The bound f(x)<=-D/(4a)<0 from the completed-square calculation holds for every x in R, including the vertex."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "R"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "52",
      "source_ref": "#/exercises/9",
      "source_pages": ["p0034#5"],
      "group_id": "g1",
      "tasks": [
        {
          "id": "52a",
          "problem": "(x^2+x-56)/(49-14x+x^2)",
          "original_domain": "x in R excluding {7}",
          "method": [
            "Factor the original numerator: x^2+x-56=(x+8)(x-7).",
            "Factor the original denominator: 49-14x+x^2=(x-7)^2, so the original formula requires x!=7.",
            "Only on this original domain, cancel one factor x-7 to obtain (x+8)/(x-7).",
            "One denominator factor remains. The original excluded value x=7 is still excluded and is a pole, not a restored value."
          ],
          "exact_answer": "(x+8)/(x-7), x!=7",
          "original_denominator_holes": ["x=7"],
          "canceled_factors": ["one factor x-7"]
        },
        {
          "id": "52b",
          "problem": "((2y-3)^2-1)/(y^2+7y-8)",
          "original_domain": "y in R excluding {-8,1}",
          "method": [
            "Use the difference of two squares: (2y-3)^2-1=(2y-4)(2y-2)=4(y-2)(y-1).",
